                <pb facs="0584" n="496" sameAs="frus:frus1933v04/pg496"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">721.23/1395: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Switzerland</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Wilson</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Geneva</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1933-03-10T19:00:00+01:00">March 10, 1933—7
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received March 10—3:20 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>144. My 140, March 7, 1 p.m. Drummond tells me that he has had a number of talks
                    in the last few days with the Peruvian delegate and he, Drummond, has the
                    impression now that the Peruvians are hesitant as to the course to pursue and
                    are particularly apprehensive at the thought of sanctions. This leads him to
                    express the opinion to me that it would be particularly useful if he could have
                    any indication as to how we would view the possibility of an embargo on arms
                    against Peru. He considers the mere possibility of such action would be
                    sufficient to turn the tide with the Peruvian Government and make them accept
                    the League’s proposal. Further, any pressure that we could bring with the
                    Peruvian Government while they are in this undecided attitude would be most
                    timely.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Wilson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
